oductive when the binding constraint is an external rate-limited dependency.\n\n4. RETRY POLICY BEHAVIOR AND EXPERIMENTATION\nThe system retry policy utilized exponential backoff with up to three retries per task, without adaptive back-off, circuit breaking, or concurrency-sensitive throttling. This static retry configuration directly aggravated downstream rejection. The pivotal experiment occurred at 09:26, when engineering teams temporarily disabled retries entirely for dependency B. The impact was immediate and dramatic: queue depth dropped by 38% in six minutes. Without retries, tasks that received HTTP 429 responses failed immediately and exited the worker pool, freeing workers to pull fresh work and process tasks that did not depend on dependency B or that slipped through available downstream capacity. Although this caused an increase in direct task failure rates, it definitively confirmed that the persistence of backoff retries within the worker loops was the dominant force driving queue stagnation and worker lockup.\n\n5. RESOLUTION AND SYSTEM RECOVERY\nFull recovery materialized only after resolving the primary external constraint. At 09:35, the rate limits on outbound dependency B were raised, causing HTTP 429 rejection rates to collapse from 31% to below 1%. With the external bottleneck removed, workers successfully completed downstream interactions on the first attempt without generating retry amplification. Consequently, the accumulated queue depth dissipated swiftly, returning below 150 tasks within eleven minutes. The rapid drain following the rate limit expansion verifies that queue clearance was strictly governed by downstream throughput rather than local worker availability or compute resources.\n\n6. EVALUATION OF ALTERNATIVE HYPOTHESES\nA competing hypothesis is that an unobserved burst of inbound workload arrived prior to 09:10, overwhelming the ingest tier and creating an initial queue spike that subsequently saturated dependency B. While this alternative hypothesis accounts for the chronological queue rise preceding the logged 09:15 dependency B error log, it is less supported as a standalone root cause. First, host compute and I/O utilization remained modest at 43% CPU and 22% disk, which is incompatible with an unmanageable internal ingestion burst. Second, had inbound volume alone caused the disruption, scaling workers from 8 to 16 would have accelerated processing and caused a meaningful queue drain, rather than a negligible 6% dip. Third, disabling retries at 09:26 would not have collapsed queue depth by 38% if gross ingest volume were the primary bottleneck. Therefore, while incoming traffic may have contributed to triggering the initial rate threshold, the rate-limiting of dependency B coupled with retry amplification was the definitive mechanism sustaining the incident.\n\n7. STAGED REMEDIATION PLAN\nTo prevent recurrence, a four-stage remediation path must be deployed. Stage 1 requires configuring a dynamic circuit breaker and dead-letter queue for dependency B to shed load instantly upon receiving consecutive 429 status codes. Stage 2 mandates redesigning the retry logic to incorporate decorrelated jitter, adaptive backoff, and hard token-bucket rate limiters aligned with downstream quotas. Stage 3 introduces backpressure controls between the queue consumer and worker dispatch tiers to avoid worker starvation during downstream brownouts. Stage 4 implements dynamic contract negotiation with dependency B administrators alongside automated alerting on 429 thresholds.",
